  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nA 500mb disturbance over the Northern Plains will dive southeastward \nacross the Midwest today and into the Ohio Valley this weekend. This \nwill prompt a cold front to push towards our region today into \ntonight, passing through most of the region Saturday morning. A \nplume of higher moisture (pwats over 2\") will surge into the region \nahead of the boundary. At the same time a ribbon of higher \ninstability (MLCAPE of 2000+ j/kg) will develop across Missouri by \nlater this afternoon and early evening along with 0-6km shear \nincreasing to 30-35 kts. Thus the environment will be supportive of \na few severe storms, with CAMS suggesting convection to develop to \nour west and move in later this evening. It does appear to be a \nrather short window for severe potential though, maybe only 2-3 \nhours, before conditions become less favorable towards midnight and \nbeyond. While the primary severe threat is across southeast MO, \nthere is at least some lower potential further east (particularly if \nstorms move in quicker this evening). While many areas may only \nreceive 0.25-0.50\" from this system, there is the potential for some \nheavier swaths in excess of 2\" (as depicted by a couple of CAMs) \nwhich could produce localized flooding.\n\nWith the cold front pushing through much of the area Saturday \nmorning, the primary threat for severe storms is most likely to be \nto our east across central KY. However, if the system slows down \njust enough we may have a small window late morning into early \nafternoon for a few stronger storms across the KY pennyrile. The \ntrend has been a bit faster the past 24 hours though.\n\nAs the upper low spins southeastward from Central Illinois Saturday \nafternoon into the Ohio Valley on Sunday, scattered showers and a \nfew isolated storms will persist across our region. Models continue \nto struggle to determine the placement of the upper low by Mon-Tues \nthough, with some suggesting primarily dry conditions for our area \nand others keeping daily rain chances in play with a further west \nsolution. \n\nLREF probabilities for MaxT remaining below 80 degrees are 40-80% \nalong and north of the Ohio River on Saturday and 50-70% for most \nareas east of the Mississippi River on Sunday. Thus, we could \nthreaten a few record cold highs, which are in the mid to upper 70s \nat our climate sites. It will certainly be a rather chilly weekend \nby August standards! Temperatures will moderate early to mid next \nweek, with highs returning to the mid to upper 80s. Probabilities \nfor reaching 90 exceed 50% across southeast MO by Wednesday and most \nof the region by Thu/Fri.",
